WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.131
A small bakery pays six hundred dollars rent each month, however many cakes it sells. On a break-even chart, this fixed cost is a flat line.

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:20.025
Each cake also needs two dollars of ingredients. Add these variable costs to the rent and you get total costs, a line that starts at six hundred and rises.

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:27.363
Each cake sells for five dollars. Total revenue starts at zero, because no sales means no money in.

00:00:27.950 --> 00:00:36.654
Where total revenue crosses total costs is break-even: two hundred cakes. Below it, the bakery makes a loss. Above it, a profit.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:47.848
Some questions ask what happens when something changes. Higher fixed costs or higher variable costs raise break-even. A higher selling price lowers it.

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:57.083
Break-even is useful. It shows how many units must be sold, lets managers ask what if questions, and supports a loan application.

00:00:57.650 --> 00:01:06.695
But it has limits. It assumes everything made is sold, that costs and revenue rise in straight lines, and its figures are only predictions.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:20.195
Here is a real question from November 2025, Paper two. Explain one limitation of a break-even chart. It is worth three marks: one limitation, then two linked steps.

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:26.083
Mark one: the limitation. A break-even chart assumes every unit made is sold.

00:01:26.650 --> 00:01:35.375
Mark two: why that is unrealistic. Samsung may not sell all its televisions, for example if a rival launches a cheaper model.

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:47.278
Mark three: the consequence. Real revenue is lower than the chart shows, so Samsung may need more sales to break even and could plan output on figures that are too optimistic.

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:56.592
A common mistake is putting break-even where revenue meets the fixed cost line. Break-even is where revenue meets total costs.

00:01:57.150 --> 00:02:02.910
Another is getting the direction wrong. A higher price does not raise break-even. It lowers it.

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:20.114
Now you try. A bike repair shop has fixed costs of one thousand two hundred dollars a month and spends eight dollars on parts per repair. It raises its price from twenty to twenty-five dollars. Does break-even rise or fall? Pause and decide.

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:34.775
Break-even falls. Before, each repair contributes twelve dollars, so it needs one hundred repairs. After, each contributes seventeen dollars, so seventy point six, rounded up to seventy-one repairs.

00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:45.505
For Explain and Analyse answers, name the use or the limit, link it to the business, then say how it affects its decision. Now try the practice questions.
